  "output": "I have a full picture now. Here's the complete performance analysis:\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\nThis is a Rails 8 app — no React, so the re-render and memory-leak questions don't apply. The real issues are in DB queries, indexes, and redundant computation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Missing Database Indexes (Highest Impact)\n\n`conversions` only has an index on `user_id`. Every date-range, status, and tool_name query is a full table scan.\n\n**`db/schema.rb`** — `conversions` table needs:\n```ruby\n# Missing indexes — all hit in admin dashboard, cleanup job, analytics\nadd_index :conversions, :created_at\nadd_index :conversions, :status\nadd_index :conversions, :tool_name\n```\n\n`users` similarly:\n```ruby\nadd_index :users, :plan\nadd_index :users, :created_at\n```\n\nMigration:\n```ruby\nclass AddPerformanceIndexes < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.1]\n  def change\n    add_index :conversions, :created_at\n    add_index :conversions, :status\n    add_index :conversions, :tool_name\n    add_index :users, :plan\n    add_index :users, :created_at\n  end\nend\n```\n\nThese immediately benefit `Admin::DashboardController` (10+ COUNT queries), `CleanupConversionsJob`, and `AnalyticsController`.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unbounded Query — Conversions Index (`conversions_controller.rb:5`)\n\n```ruby\n# BAD — loads every conversion the user has ever made\n@conversions = current_user.conversions.order(created_at: :desc)\n```\n\n```ruby\n# GOOD — paginate; 20 rows per page\n@conversions = current_user.conversions.order(created_at: :desc).limit(20).offset(params.fetch(:page, 0).to_i * 20)\n```\n\nOr if you already have Kaminari in Gemfile (check): `.page(params[:page]).per(20)`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Repeated ToolRegistry Linear Scan — `conversions/index.html.erb:36`\n\nEvery row in the conversions table triggers a full O(n) scan of the 25-tool array:\n\n```erb\n<%# BAD — O(25) scan per row %>\n<% tool_meta = ToolRegistry.tools.find { |t| t[:class_name] == conversion.tool_name } %>\n```\n\nFix in `tool_registry.rb` — add a hash index by `class_name`:\n\n```ruby\ndef self.by_class_name\n  @by_class_name ||= tools.index_by { |t| t[:class_name] }\nend\n\ndef self.find(tool_id)\n  @by_id ||= tools.index_by { |t| t[:id] }\n  @by_id[tool_id]\nend\n\ndef self.categories\n  @categories ||= tools.group_by { |t| t[:category] }\nend\n```\n\nThen in the view:\n```erb\n<% tool_meta = ToolRegistry.by_class_name[conversion.tool_name] %>\n```\n\nAnd in `conversions_controller.rb:39`:\n```ruby\n@tool_meta = ToolRegistry.by_class_name[@conversion.tool_name]\n```\n\n`@categories ||=` also fixes the per-request recomputation in `ToolsController#index`.\n\n> **Note:** `||=` on a class-level `cattr_accessor` is process-local — safe since the tool list is static. If tools ever become DB-driven, swap for `Rails.cache.fetch`.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Admin Dashboard: 15 Queries, No Caching (`admin/dashboard_controller.rb`)\n\nEvery admin page load fires ~15 COUNT queries. Add a short cache:\n\n```ruby\ndef index\n  # ponytail: 5-min cache, bust it if you need fresh admin data\n  stats = Rails.cache.fetch(\"admin_dashboard_stats\", expires_in: 5.minutes) do\n    now = Time.current\n    {\n      total_users:           User.count,\n      users_today:           User.where(created_at: now.beginning_of_day..).count,\n      users_this_week:       User.where(created_at: 7.days.ago..).count,\n      users_this_month:      User.where(created_at: 30.days.ago..).count,\n      confirmed_users:       User.where.not(confirmed_at: nil).count,\n      unconfirmed_users:     User.where(confirmed_at: nil).count,\n      google_users:          User.where(provider: \"google_oauth2\").count,\n      email_users:           User.where(provider: nil).count,\n      pro_users:             User.where(plan: :pro).count,\n      free_users:            User.where(plan: :free).count,\n      total_conversions:     Conversion.count,\n      conversions_today:     Conversion.where(created_at: now.beginning_of_day..).count,\n      conversions_this_week: Conversion.where(created_at: 7.days.ago..).count,\n      completed_conversions: Conversion.where(status: :completed).count,\n      failed_conversions:    Conversion.where(status: :failed).count,\n      top_tools:             Conversion.where(created_at: 30.days.ago..).group(:tool_name).order(\"count_all DESC\").limit(10).count,\n      conversions_by_day:    Conversion.where(created_at: 30.days.ago..).group(\"DATE(created_at)\").order(\"DATE(created_at)\").count,\n      users_by_day:          User.where(created_at: 30.days.ago..).group(\"DATE(created_at)\").order(\"DATE(created_at)\").count\n    }\n  end\n\n  stats.each { |k, v| instance_variable_set(\"@#{k}\", v) }\n\n  # These are cheap enough to not cache (small LIMIT)\n  @recent_users       = User.order(created_at: :desc).limit(10)\n  @recent_conversions = Conversion.includes(:user).order(created_at: :desc).limit(10)\nend\n```\n\nYou already have `solid_cache_entries` in the schema, so `Rails.cache` is wired up — zero extra deps.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Analytics: 3 Queries → 1 (`analytics_controller.rb:6-11`)\n\n```ruby\n# BAD — 3 separate DB round-trips\n@total_conversions = current_user.conversions.count\n@recent_conversions = current_user.conversions.where('created_at > ?', 30.days.ago).count\n@older_conversions  = current_user.conversions.where('created_at BETWEEN ? AND ?', 60.days.ago, 30.days.ago).count\n```\n\n```ruby\n# GOOD — 1 query with conditional counts\ncounts = current_user.conversions\n  .select(\n    \"COUNT(*) AS total,\n     COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE created_at > :recent)    AS recent,\n     COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE created_at BETWEEN :older AND :recent) AS older\",\n    recent: 30.days.ago, older: 60.days.ago\n  ).first\n\n@total_conversions  = counts.total.to_i\n@recent_conversions = counts.recent.to_i\n@older_conversions  = counts.older.to_i\n```\n\n> PostgreSQL-only (`FILTER` clause) — valid since this app uses Postgres per the schema.\n\n---\n\n### 6. ToolRunner: Two `ConversionLimiter` Instances (`tool_runner.rb:21,50`)\n\n```ruby\n# BAD — two separate objects, conversions_count accessed twice\ndef validate_limits!\n  limiter = ConversionLimiter.new(@user)  # instance 1\n  raise LimitExceededError, limiter.status_message unless limiter.can_convert?\nend\n\ndef run!\n  # ...\n  ConversionLimiter.new(@user).increment!  # instance 2\nend\n```\n\n```ruby\n# GOOD — one memoized limiter\ndef limiter\n  @limiter ||= ConversionLimiter.new(@user)\nend\n\ndef validate_limits!\n  raise LimitExceededError, limiter.status_message unless limiter.can_convert?\nend\n\ndef run!\n  # ...\n  limiter.increment!\nend\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary by Priority\n\n| # | Issue | File | Impact |\n|---|-------|------|--------|\n| 1 | Missing indexes on `conversions.created_at/status/tool_name`, `users.plan/created_at` | `db/schema.rb` | **High** — every admin/analytics query is a full scan |\n| 2 | Unbounded conversions query | `conversions_controller.rb:5` | **High** — grows forever per user |\n| 3 | ToolRegistry linear scan in view loop | `conversions/index.html.erb:36` | **Medium** — O(25) per row, add hash index |\n| 4 | Admin dashboard: 15+ uncached queries | `admin/dashboard_controller.rb` | **Medium** — 5-min cache with existing SolidCache |\n| 5 | Analytics: 3 queries → 1 | `analytics_controller.rb:6-11` | **Low** — simple consolidation |\n| 6 | Duplicate `ConversionLimiter` | `tool_runner.rb:21,50` | **Low** — cosmetic/minor |\n\nStart with **#1 (indexes)** — it's a 5-line migration that improves every query in the codebase that touches those columns.\n",
